⟦P0⟧⟦P1⟧ 140-3 Compliance⟦P2⟧Starting with Go ⟦P3⟧, Go binaries can natively operate in a mode that⟦P4⟧facilitates ⟦P5⟧ 140-3 compliance. Moreover, the toolchain can build against⟦P6⟧frozen versions of the cryptography packages that constitute the Go⟦P7⟧Cryptographic Module.⟦P8⟧⟦P9⟧ ⟦P10⟧ 140-3 is a ⟦P11⟧. Government compliance regime for cryptography⟦P12⟧applications that amongst other things requires the use of a set of approved⟦P13⟧algorithms, and the use of⟦P14⟧⟦P15⟧⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧-validated⟦P18⟧cryptographic modules tested in the target operating environments.⟦P19⟧The mechanisms described in this page facilitate compliance for Go applications.⟦P20⟧Applications that have no need for ⟦P21⟧ 140-3 compliance can safely ignore them,⟦P22⟧and should not enable ⟦P23⟧ 140-3 mode.⟦P24⟧⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧:⟦P27⟧ Simply using a ⟦P28⟧ 140-3 compliant and validated cryptographic module⟦P29⟧may not—on its own—satisfy all relevant regulatory requirements. The Go team cannot provide any⟦P30⟧guarantees or support around how usage of the provided ⟦P31⟧ 140-3⟦P32⟧mode may, or may not, satisfy specific regulatory requirements for individual⟦P33⟧users. Care should be taken in determining if usage of this module satisfies⟦P34⟧your specific requirements.⟦P35⟧The Go Cryptographic Module⟦P36⟧The Go Cryptographic Module is a collection of standard library Go packages⟦P37⟧under ⟦P38⟧ that implement ⟦P39⟧ 140-3 approved⟦P40⟧algorithms.⟦P41⟧Public ⟦P42⟧ packages such as ⟦P43⟧ and ⟦P44⟧ transparently use⟦P45⟧the Go Cryptographic Module to implement ⟦P46⟧ 140-3 algorithms.⟦P47⟧⟦P48⟧ 140-3 mode⟦P49⟧When operating in ⟦P50⟧ 140-3 mode:⟦P51⟧The Go Cryptographic Module automatically performs an integrity self-check at⟦P52⟧⟦P53⟧ time, comparing the checksum of the module's object file computed at⟦P54⟧build time with the symbols loaded in memory.⟦P55⟧All algorithms perform known-answer self-tests according to the relevant ⟦P56⟧⟦P57⟧140-3 Implementation Guidance, either at ⟦P58⟧ time, or on first use.⟦P59⟧Pairwise consistency tests are performed on generated cryptographic keys.⟦P60⟧Note that this can cause a slowdown of up to 2x for certain key types, which⟦P61⟧is especially relevant for ephemeral keys.⟦P62⟧⟦P63⟧⟦P64⟧⟦P65⟧ is implemented in terms of a⟦P66⟧⟦P67⟧ ⟦P68⟧ ⟦P69⟧ ⟦P70⟧. To guarantee the same level of security as programs not⟦P71⟧running in ⟦P72⟧ 140-3 mode, random bytes are also sourced from the platform's⟦P73⟧⟦P74⟧ at every ⟦P75⟧ and mixed into the output as uncredited additional⟦P76⟧data.⟦P77⟧The ⟦P78⟧⟦P79⟧⟦P80⟧ package will ignore and not negotiate⟦P81⟧any protocol version, cipher suite, signature algorithm, or key exchange⟦P82⟧mechanism that is not ⟦P83⟧ 140-3 approved. (This is equivalent to the legacy⟦P84⟧opt-in ⟦P85⟧ ⟦P86⟧ mechanism.)⟦P87⟧⟦P88⟧⟦P89⟧⟦P90⟧ with⟦P91⟧⟦P92⟧⟦P93⟧⟦P94⟧ will cap the length⟦P95⟧of the salt at the length of the hash.⟦P96⟧⟦P97⟧ 140-3 mode is not supported on ⟦P98⟧, Wasm, ⟦P99⟧, and 32-bit Windows.⟦P100⟧The ⟦P101⟧ package⟦P102⟧The ⟦P103⟧⟦P104⟧⟦P105⟧ function reports⟦P106⟧whether ⟦P107⟧ 140-3 mode is active.⟦P108⟧The ⟦P109⟧⟦P110⟧⟦P111⟧ function returns⟦P112⟧the version of the Go Cryptographic Module in use.⟦P113⟧The ⟦P114⟧ environment variable⟦P115⟧The ⟦P116⟧ environment variable can be used with ⟦P117⟧, ⟦P118⟧,⟦P119⟧and ⟦P120⟧ to select the version of the Go Cryptographic Module to be linked⟦P121⟧into the executable program, and to enable ⟦P122⟧ 140-3 mode by default.⟦P123⟧⟦P124⟧ is the default, and uses the ⟦P125⟧ packages in⟦P126⟧the standard library tree in use.⟦P127⟧⟦P128⟧ is like ⟦P129⟧, but enables ⟦P130⟧ 140-3 mode by default.⟦P131⟧⟦P132⟧ or ⟦P133⟧ select the specific respective Go Cryptographic Module⟦P134⟧versions. They enable ⟦P135⟧ 140-3 mode by default.⟦P136⟧⟦P137⟧ and ⟦P138⟧ are equivalent to specifying the latest version⟦P139⟧that reached the ⟦P140⟧⟦P141⟧ Modules In Process List⟦P142⟧ and the latest version that⟦P143⟧obtained a ⟦P144⟧⟦P145⟧ validation certificate⟦P146⟧, respectively.⟦P147⟧The ⟦P148⟧ ⟦P149⟧ option⟦P150⟧The run-time ⟦P151⟧ ⟦P152⟧⟦P153⟧⟦P154⟧ option controls whether the Go⟦P155⟧Cryptographic Module operates in ⟦P156⟧ 140-3 mode. It can't be changed after the⟦P157⟧program has started.⟦P158⟧It defaults to ⟦P159⟧ unless ⟦P160⟧ is set at build time.⟦P161⟧If set to ⟦P162⟧, ⟦P163⟧ 140-3 mode is enabled. This is possible even if ⟦P164⟧⟦P165⟧was not set at build time.⟦P166⟧If set to ⟦P167⟧, cryptographic algorithms that are not ⟦P168⟧ 140-3 compliant⟦P169⟧will return an error or panic. Note that this is a best effort mode meant for⟦P170⟧testing, assessment, and debugging. ⟦P171⟧It is not intended to be used in⟦P172⟧production⟦P173⟧, it is not required by the Security Policy, it introduces crashes⟦P174⟧and potentially unhandled errors by design, and it may have false positives or⟦P175⟧false negatives.⟦P176⟧Most programs should not set this option directly, and should instead use⟦P177⟧⟦P178⟧ at build time.⟦P179⟧Module versions, validations, and compatibility⟦P180⟧Google currently has a contractual relationship with ⟦P181⟧Geomys⟦P182⟧⟦P183⟧to facilitate at least yearly ⟦P184⟧ validations of the Go Cryptographic Module.⟦P185⟧At the time of validation we will freeze the Go Cryptographic Module and create⟦P186⟧a new module version for submission.⟦P187⟧These validations are tested on a comprehensive set of Operating⟦P188⟧Environments, supporting many popular operating system and hardware platform⟦P189⟧combinations.⟦P190⟧Older Go Cryptographic Module versions continue to be supported and available⟦P191⟧for as long as a more recent version has not obtained a ⟦P192⟧ validation⟦P193⟧certificate. Once a more recent version has obtained a ⟦P194⟧ validation⟦P195⟧certificate, older versions will be removed.⟦P196⟧Some standard library features may be unavailable and return errors if using a⟦P197⟧Go Cryptographic Module that was frozen from an older version of Go.⟦P198⟧Go Cryptographic Module ⟦P199⟧⟦P200⟧Go Cryptographic Module ⟦P201⟧ was frozen in early 2026 from Go ⟦P202⟧.⟦P203⟧It is available in Go ⟦P204⟧.⟦P205⟧As of 2026-04-28, it is Pending Review in the ⟦P206⟧ Modules In Process List.⟦P207⟧It is covered by ⟦P208⟧⟦P209⟧ Certificate ⟦P210⟧⟦P211⟧.⟦P212⟧Changes from ⟦P213⟧⟦P214⟧Implemented ⟦P215⟧.⟦P216⟧⟦P217⟧⟦P218⟧⟦P219⟧ is now supported.⟦P220⟧Introduced new ⟦P221⟧ compliance APIs, for use in ⟦P222⟧ and future exposed APIs.⟦P223⟧The Go Cryptographic Module now uses a ⟦P224⟧ jitter Entropy Source, with⟦P225⟧⟦P226⟧⟦P227⟧ Certificate ⟦P228⟧⟦P229⟧⟦P230⟧and ⟦P231⟧⟦P232⟧ Certificate ⟦P233⟧⟦P234⟧.⟦P235⟧(The platform ⟦P236⟧ is still used as an uncredited additional data source for all random bytes.)⟦P237⟧Various safety and performance improvements.⟦P238⟧Go Cryptographic Module ⟦P239⟧⟦P240⟧Go Cryptographic Module ⟦P241⟧ was frozen in early 2024 from Go ⟦P242⟧.⟦P243⟧It is available in Go ⟦P244⟧.⟦P245⟧It is covered by ⟦P246⟧⟦P247⟧ Certificate #5247⟦P248⟧⟦P249⟧and ⟦P250⟧⟦P251⟧ Certificate ⟦P252⟧⟦P253⟧.⟦P254⟧The previous, unsupported mechanism to use the ⟦P255⟧ module for certain⟦P256⟧⟦P257⟧ 140-3 approved algorithms is currently still available, but it is meant to⟦P258⟧be removed and replaced with the mechanism described in this page in a future⟦P259⟧release.⟦P260⟧⟦P261⟧ is incompatible with the native ⟦P262⟧ 140-3 mode.⟦P263⟧
